{
  "title": "QUADRATICS,\n(FACTORISING QUADRATICS)",
  "objective": "LO: I can factorise quadratic expressions of the form x&sup2; + bx + c and ax&sup2; + bx + c.",
  "send_mode": false,
  "info_boxes": {
    "key_terms": {
      "id": "key_terms",
      "title": "Factorising quadratics",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "<span class=\"ul-b\">Factorising</span> a quadratic means writing it as a product of two brackets. For x&sup2; + bx + c, find two numbers that <span class=\"ul-k\">multiply to c</span> and <span class=\"ul-k\">add to b</span>."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "<span class=\"rb\">Strategy:</span> List factor pairs of c. Find the pair that sums to b. Write in two brackets."
        }
      ]
    },
    "what_we_learn": {
      "id": "what_we_learn",
      "title": "Quick examples",
      "examples": [
        {
          "correct": true,
          "expr": "x&sup2; + 7x + 12 = (x + 3)(x + 4)",
          "desc": "3 &times; 4 = 12, 3 + 4 = 7"
        },
        {
          "correct": true,
          "expr": "x&sup2; &minus; 5x + 6 = (x &minus; 2)(x &minus; 3)",
          "desc": "&minus;2 &times; &minus;3 = 6, &minus;2 + &minus;3 = &minus;5"
        },
        {
          "correct": true,
          "expr": "x&sup2; + x &minus; 12 = (x + 4)(x &minus; 3)",
          "desc": "4 &times; &minus;3 = &minus;12, 4 + &minus;3 = 1"
        },
        {
          "correct": false,
          "expr": "x&sup2; + 7x + 12 = (x + 6)(x + 2)",
          "desc": "6 + 2 = 8, not 7"
        },
        {
          "correct": false,
          "expr": "x&sup2; &minus; 5x + 6 = (x &minus; 6)(x + 1)",
          "desc": "&minus;6 &times; 1 = &minus;6, not +6"
        }
      ]
    },
    "key_idea": {
      "id": "key_idea",
      "title": "Key idea",
      "text": "Find two numbers that multiply to c and add to b, then write x&sup2; + bx + c = (x + m)(x + n).",
      "equation": "x&sup2; + bx + c = (x + <span class=\"ov\">m</span>)(x + <span class=\"ovr\">n</span>) where mn = c and m + n = b",
      "caption": "Product = c, Sum = b"
    }
  },
  "modelled_examples": [
    {
      "id": "ex1",
      "card_color": "#1F5FA6",
      "card_bg": "#EEF3FF",
      "label": "Example 1 &ndash; both positive",
      "question": "Factorise x&sup2; + 8x + 15",
      "steps": [
        "Need two numbers: multiply to 15, add to 8",
        "Factor pairs of 15: 1&times;15, 3&times;5",
        "3 + 5 = 8, so (x + 3)(x + 5)"
      ],
      "answer": "(x + 3)(x + 5)",
      "explanation": "Both numbers positive because b and c are both positive."
    },
    {
      "id": "ex2",
      "card_color": "#1F5FA6",
      "card_bg": "#EEF3FF",
      "label": "Example 2 &ndash; both negative",
      "question": "Factorise x&sup2; &minus; 7x + 12",
      "steps": [
        "Need: multiply to +12, add to &minus;7",
        "Both must be negative: &minus;3 &times; &minus;4 = 12, &minus;3 + &minus;4 = &minus;7",
        "= (x &minus; 3)(x &minus; 4)"
      ],
      "answer": "(x &minus; 3)(x &minus; 4)",
      "explanation": "c positive but b negative means both numbers are negative."
    },
    {
      "id": "ex3",
      "card_color": "#CC0000",
      "card_bg": "#FFF0F0",
      "label": "Example 3 &ndash; mixed signs",
      "question": "Factorise x&sup2; + 2x &minus; 15",
      "steps": [
        "Need: multiply to &minus;15, add to +2",
        "Try: 5 &times; (&minus;3) = &minus;15, 5 + (&minus;3) = 2",
        "= (x + 5)(x &minus; 3)"
      ],
      "answer": "(x + 5)(x &minus; 3)",
      "explanation": "c negative means one number positive, one negative."
    },
    {
      "id": "ex4",
      "card_color": "#1E7D2E",
      "card_bg": "#EDFAEE",
      "label": "Example 4 &ndash; coefficient of x&sup2; not 1",
      "question": "Factorise 2x&sup2; + 7x + 3",
      "steps": [
        "Multiply a &times; c = 2 &times; 3 = 6. Find pair: product 6, sum 7",
        "6 and 1: split middle term: 2x&sup2; + 6x + x + 3",
        "Factorise in pairs: 2x(x+3) + 1(x+3) = (2x+1)(x+3)"
      ],
      "answer": "(2x + 1)(x + 3)",
      "explanation": "Use the grouping method when a &ne; 1."
    }
  ],
